This approach is useful for understanding the reconstruction process, but we can improve it.

Approach 2 — Recursive Ranges + Linear Search
Idea
Instead of creating new arrays, use index ranges to identify the current subtree.

Each recursive call receives:
preStart, preEnd
inStart, inEnd


These define the relevant sections of the original preorder and inorder arrays.

The procedure is:
1. If the range is empty, return null.
2. preorder[preStart] is the root.
3. Find the root's position in the current inorder range.
4. Compute the number of nodes in the left subtree.
5. Recursively construct left and right subtrees using index ranges.
The key formula is:
\[
\text{leftSize}=\text{rootIndex}-\text{inStart}
\]

Code
class Solution {

    public TreeNode buildTree(int[] preorder, int[] inorder) {

        return build(
                preorder, 0, preorder.length - 1,
                inorder, 0, inorder.length - 1
        );
    }

    private TreeNode build(
            int[] preorder,
            int preStart,
            int preEnd,
            int[] inorder,
            int inStart,
            int inEnd) {

        if (preStart > preEnd || inStart > inEnd) {
            return null;
        }

        int rootValue = preorder[preStart];
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(rootValue);

        // Linear search inside the current inorder range
        int rootIndex = inStart;

        while (rootIndex <= inEnd
                && inorder[rootIndex] != rootValue) {
            rootIndex++;
        }

        int leftSize = rootIndex - inStart;

        root.left = build(
                preorder,
                preStart + 1,
                preStart + leftSize,
                inorder,
                inStart,
                rootIndex - 1
        );

        root.right = build(
                preorder,
                preStart + leftSize + 1,
                preEnd,
                inorder,
                rootIndex + 1,
                inEnd
        );

        return root;
    }
}


