Approach 4 — Iterative Stack (Optimal Time)
A recursive solution isn't the only way to achieve \(O(n)\) time. We can also reconstruct the tree using a stack and an inorder pointer.
Idea
Preorder tells us the next node to create. Inorder tells us when we've completed a subtree.

Maintain:
- A stack of nodes whose right children have not yet been determined.
- A pointer inorderIndex indicating the next inorder value to match.
For each new preorder value:
1. If the stack top does not match the current inorder value, it must become the top node's left child.
2. Otherwise, pop all stack nodes matching inorder, advancing the inorder pointer. The last popped node receives the new value as its right child.
Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public TreeNode buildTree(int[] preorder, int[] inorder) {

        if (preorder.length == 0) {
            return null;
        }

        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(preorder[0]);

        Stack<TreeNode> stack = new Stack<>();
        stack.push(root);

        int inorderIndex = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < preorder.length; i++) {

            TreeNode curr = stack.peek();

            TreeNode node = new TreeNode(preorder[i]);

            // We have not finished the current node's left subtree.
            if (curr.val != inorder[inorderIndex]) {

                curr.left = node;

            } else {

                // Finish nodes that now match inorder.
                while (!stack.isEmpty()
                        && inorderIndex < inorder.length
                        && stack.peek().val == inorder[inorderIndex]) {

                    curr = stack.pop();
                    inorderIndex++;
                }

                // The next preorder node is the right child
                // of the last node completed by inorder.
                curr.right = node;
            }

            stack.push(node);
        }

        return root;
    }
}



